WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.480
So let's continue our journey
into the image compression.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.200
The next few lectures will be
a little bit more advanced.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.380
We won't have any specific
homeworks on them,

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.750
but please try to take in as
much as you can in the class.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.120
Be participative,
have discussions

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.890
around it, and everything, OK?

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:28.390
Cool.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:29.290
Let's get started.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.619
So just a quick recap, like,
what is an image, right?

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.938
So you have an array
of pixels, which

00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:36.730
is, like, this height,
width, and channels.

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.700
And typically, you
would have-- you

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.500
will store, like, each
of these values as,

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.660
like, 1 byte or sometimes
10 bits or 12 bits, right?

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.710
And then we looked
at, like, what happens

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:49.960
when you do image compression.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.790
So it has been, I
guess, a long week's

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.190
break, so I'm just
going to reproduce

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.650
these slides from the last
time just so that you guys can

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:58.300
refresh what we saw.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.070
And so this is a raw image.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.880
And this takes like 1.1
megabyte, uncompressed.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.300
And if you compress
it using JPEG by 40x,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.307
so this file becomes,
like, 27 kilobytes.

00:01:09.307 --> 00:01:11.140
And if I go back and
forth, you don't really

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.610
see any difference.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:17.110
So lossless-- sorry, lossy
image compression with JPEG

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.550
seems to be doing fine.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.795
But now, like, we have studied
about the rate distortion.

00:01:21.795 --> 00:01:23.170
So you can ask
the question like,

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.760
what happens if I
keep compressing it

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:25.690
further and further?

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.800
So if you compress it
further at this point,

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.400
you can start seeing
some noticeable artifacts

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:31.300
in the image.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.940
So this file is at
14 kB at this point,

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:38.890
but you can see quite a high
distortion so as to say, right?

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.510
And if you go even
further, if you

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.570
try to compress this to, like,
by two orders of magnitude,

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:48.030
8 kilobytes, now this picture
is completely unrecognizable.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.610
And then we saw last
class that-- oh, but--

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.970
and we learned a lot about JPEG
like how JPEG exactly works.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.550
But then towards the end, we
also just briefly touched upon,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.530
like, why JPEG is like--

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.660
there are many places
where JPEG can be improved.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.570
Like JPEG is not really
an upper bound so as

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.160
to say in terms of performance.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.580
And we learned something about
[? a ?] compressor called BPG.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.780
What BPG does is just allows you
to have variable block sizes as

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.670
well as predictive coding.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.950
And we had a quiz question
on predictive coding

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.300
just to get you guys practice.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.550
Like I said, it's nothing
tough, just very obvious steps.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.790
And so if you compare the
performance of JPEG with BPG,

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.980
so now both of them
are at 8 kilobytes.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.990
But as you can see like--
oops, OK. yeah, as you can see,

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.660
there's, like, a big difference
in the reconstructed image,

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:37.160
right?

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.800
So obviously, like, JPEG can
be improved by quite a lot.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.660
It's very obvious.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:47.580
But BPG still uses, like, a
lot of hand-tuned parameters.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.062
And we'll come to
that in a second.

00:02:49.062 --> 00:02:51.270
And that's what today's
lecture is going to be about.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.130
Like how can you go
even beyond, like,

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.410
the traditional
image compressors?

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.450
So with this revision
just to go over,

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.490
like, the quiz
questions quickly,

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.800
so the first question
was just, like,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.160
trying to understand, like,
the same basic principles.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.190
I would say just go over the
solution in your free time.

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.830
It's, like, quite
straightforward,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.680
the answers for the first three.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.510
It's just like literally
doing this weight times

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.160
height times number
of bytes math,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.020
but now we added
an alpha channel

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.070
to make things more interesting.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:28.320
And then the last question
was somewhat interesting,

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.020
which is like in this
question, we saw that PNG still

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.920
seems to perform better than
even if you were using just one

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.200
bit for color and one
bit for alpha channel,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.750
but it seems like all the
best you can do in this case.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:41.490
And the question asks like, why?

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.010
And we didn't really talk
about PNG in the class.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.590
The idea was to-- now
you know about JPEG.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:51.540
I was to guess how you can do
even better, why this lossless

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.730
compressor is performing
so much better.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.680
And there are lots
of correct answers,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.310
but the just basic idea
what we were looking for--

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:04.080
or anything is fine-- is that
PNG basically does much better

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.200
entropy coding than JPEG.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:09.870
Recall JPEG was just
doing a basic run length

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:11.850
encoding plus Huffman
encoding, but we

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.040
know that you can do--
you can do better.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.860
And in this case, it's like a
really, really uniform image.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:23.320
So PNG, which uses actually
an LZ77-based lossless

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.710
entropy coder, can perform
very well because it

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:27.820
gets you a lot of matches.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.580
So if you look back to
your homework three Pokémon

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:33.310
question, right, so that's
really the intuition.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:36.790
It's the same
intuition at play here.

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:39.050
OK, any questions?

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:43.060
Good.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.650
And then in the
second question, we

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:49.990
made this JPEE274G
compressor, right?

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:53.260
And the idea was really to
work through some of the design

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.860
decisions like what we
talked about in the class.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:01.030
The first question was
around like, when would you

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:05.350
think increasing the block size
gives you the more benefit?

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.890
And the main intuition
there is that when

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:12.340
you have the same image,
and you are representing it

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.560
in much higher
resolution, that means

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.020
you have more number
of pixels representing

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.060
the same information,
and hence there

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.740
is lesser correlation between
neighboring pixels, right?

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.960
And so when you
expand the block size,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.110
you will be able to capture
more correlation between pixels

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:30.370
within a block size.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:33.460
And that's how DCT works,
and it really helps you.

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.290
And so the answer
for the first one

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:38.560
was basically like you
expect to see more benefits

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.160
for the image with
higher resolution

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.440
as you increase the block size.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.580
And then the second question
was now on like, OK--

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:50.812
so OK, the first question--
the idea was like,

00:05:50.812 --> 00:05:52.270
what happens within
a block, right?

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.300
We were trying to see how
the correlations work out

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:56.170
within a block.

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:59.400
The second question
was like, OK, now,

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.800
we said that this
compressor also

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.190
uses prediction of blocks based
on previously encoded blocks.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:07.890
So now, instead of just looking
at pixels within a block,

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:10.230
we are trying to see if
the neighboring blocks

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:13.500
and everything-- are they
predictive of the next block?

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:17.280
And again, the idea really is if
you can do a better prediction,

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.870
then you can use that prediction
to have lower residuals

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.420
and compress your stream
much better, right?

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:29.250
This is exactly the two
ideas which BPG also uses

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:30.930
to improve upon JPEG, right?

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.330
So it allows you to
have bigger block sizes,

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.580
and it also allows you
to have prediction.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:38.370
And so the idea there
really was that, oh,

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:41.430
if you have more homogeneous
blocks, if your image is really

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.620
consisting of very
similar looking blocks,

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:46.650
you can do better predictive
coding because it's probably

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:47.760
just a copy, paste.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.432
So in this Charizard
image, you'll

00:06:50.432 --> 00:06:52.140
probably have blocks,
which are basically

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.140
all same color versus
this assorted Pokémon

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.590
image, which has lots
of things happening, OK?

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:01.650
So this is basically,
again, the ideas

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:07.040
of how BPG improves in some
sense compared to JPEG.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:08.470
Any questions?

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:13.000
No?

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:13.630
Cool.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:17.940
And then the third question
was around predictive coding.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.910
So we described an algorithm
for predictive coding.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:22.872
In class, I just
mentioned that we'll do--

00:07:22.872 --> 00:07:24.330
predictive coding
is not that hard.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.872
So if you work
through this question,

00:07:25.872 --> 00:07:27.840
you hopefully
understand the algorithm

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.340
for doing predictive coding.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.910
And then we gave just,
like, two questions,

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.400
one on decoding and one
on finding the errors.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.200
And I think it's
quite straightforward.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:39.690
But if you didn't understand,
just go through this

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:43.370
and reach out to us in
the office hours using it.

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:43.870
OK.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:47.450
Cool.

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:50.820
Any questions in the--

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.490
so far on image
compression or quizzes?

00:07:58.090 --> 00:07:58.590
Nope.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:07:59.220
OK, great.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.220
So let's continue.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.285
OK, again, JPEG at
8 kilobytes-- this

00:08:05.285 --> 00:08:06.660
is the reconstructed
image, which

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:13.230
you get BPG at 8 kilobytes,
super better than what JPEG is.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:15.210
But if you look at
it carefully, like,

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:16.860
you still see some
artifacts, right?

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:20.040
Like you see some
blockiness in this nose,

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:24.510
similarly here, some
ringing stuff, right?

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:27.300
So this is what you
get with ML-based image

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.900
compression, again, at 8
kilobytes, OK, same file size.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.840
And so if I go back and
forth between BPG and this,

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.700
can you see the difference?

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.385
So it's quite-- maybe if
you look at the girl's eye,

00:08:38.385 --> 00:08:39.360
I think--

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:39.944
oops, sorry.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:44.220
So it's quite drastic, right?

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:47.490
So the point really is OK,
BPG is also not the same.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.170
Like it's not really
an upper bound.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.090
You can still
perform much better.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.150
And again, if you have to
really take a stark contrast,

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.070
this is what JPEG
reconstruction looks at,

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:57.360
same file size versus--

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.060
this is what an ML-based
reconstruction looks.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:04.800
So there's clearly a lot
of room for improvement.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:07.680
OK, and so today we'll be
learning more about how do you,

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.600
like, employ ML and deploy
it for the use of image

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.760
compression particularly.

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:21.320
OK, so just a reminder, this is
what our pipeline for the JPEG

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:21.860
looked like.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.480
And we have seen similar things
over the past few classes,

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.020
so now you should be
comfortable with the ideas.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:28.700
You start with something LGB.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:30.050
You do color treatment.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:33.890
This is mostly to decorrelate
the natural signals.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:38.990
And then we do, I would say,
a vanilla lossy compressor

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:41.060
pipeline, which is, first,
you do some transform

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:42.860
to decorrelate your components.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.330
Then you do quantization.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.480
Finally, you do some lossless
encoding, some entropy coding,

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.460
and that gives you
your JPEG file, right?

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:51.140
And we went into
details of each of

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:54.660
these components last lecture.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.340
So now the question
is, like, how

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:59.370
can we improve this
even further, OK?

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:02.310
So the first idea really
is, like, maybe we

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.350
can go beyond linear transform.

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:09.300
So if you saw, like, JPEG,
JPEG2000, BPG, all of these

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:11.190
uses some variants
of discrete cosine

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:12.900
transform, discrete
wavelet transform,

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.370
discrete Fourier transform.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:16.860
But all of these transforms
are just linear maps.

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:19.230
It's a matrix multiplication.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.120
Maybe we can do
something better if we

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.550
allow for some nonlinear
transform coding

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:26.880
to take place, which we haven't
studied in class at all so far,

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.260
right?

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.250
And so this is really the idea.

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:35.008
So think of these plots as
the two-dimensional vector

00:10:35.008 --> 00:10:36.300
coding which we have looked at.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.510
So x-axis is just some
source dimension one.

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:42.420
Y-axis is the source dimension
two so like x1, x2 things

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:43.800
which we have been looking at.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:47.220
These purple dots are
the codebook vectors,

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:50.760
and the regions are the
codebook boundaries.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:54.060
And let's say your density
function, the actual source

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.830
which you are encoding, is
in this banana kind of shape,

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.080
where yellow color means
higher probability density,

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:01.540
and blue color means
less probability density.

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:04.420
And so if you were to
vector quantize this block,

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:06.490
and you are only
allowed to do, like,

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:09.580
linear boundaries with
this vector transform,

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:12.730
this is probably the best
you can do to capture this.

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:14.770
But if you were allowed
nonlinear boundaries,

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.080
then I can exactly
cover this vanilla--

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.120
sorry, this banana
peel kind of shape.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:22.420
And I only need to transmit
these few vectors, right?

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:24.820
And you can be much
more efficient.

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:28.210
Sorry, only these few
codebook vectors here, right?

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:32.740
And so in this very trivial,
like, synthetic example,

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:36.970
you can already see that if your
PDF of real sources-- nobody

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:38.980
said they have to follow
some linear boundaries,

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:41.200
or they can be
linearly decorrelated.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.240
Maybe there is some
nonlinear transform

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.810
which decorrelates them.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:49.240
So that's one.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:54.100
The other idea is really more
related to ML, which is--

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:55.900
so in JPEG, we saw
there were, like,

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.300
lots of these hand-determined
heuristics, right?

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:02.050
Like we had these quantization
matrices which were like--

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:03.520
which just came out of nowhere.

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:04.990
These were, like,
specific numbers.

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.240
When I say nowhere, it
isn't really nowhere.

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:09.460
We motivated it
based on NLG and lots

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:11.260
of psychovisual experiments.

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.930
But nobody said, like,
these quantization matrix

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:18.490
is the best one for your image
or your particular data set.

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:21.190
Similarly, there were, like,
other design decisions like 8

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:24.430
by 8 block size, why
not more, for example.

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:27.550
And so there are lots
of heuristics involved

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:28.750
during the design of JPEG.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:31.360
A lot of smart people
really, over the years,

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:34.810
like, fine-tune each
small knob to optimize

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:38.590
every bit, which contributes
and goes into JPEG, right?

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:40.660
But this is really hard, right?

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:43.660
Also, like, if we think
about R-D optimization, which

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:45.280
we actually didn't
talk about much--

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.270
but somehow we have to do this.

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:49.040
We have to trade off
rate with distortion.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:51.980
And the way you do it in JPEG
is by changing your quantization

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:52.940
matrix, right?

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:54.980
And again, like,
this changed also.

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:57.020
So not only the values
which were determined

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:58.820
for one particular
rate distortion,

00:12:58.820 --> 00:13:01.820
but the change between
different rates and distortion,

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:03.770
the trade-offs between
rate and distortion

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:05.660
were also
hand-determined, right?

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:09.770
And there is no way really
to know that that is optimal.

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:13.430
And so one question
really is, can we

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:16.400
motivate this decision in
a more end-to-end fashion?

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:18.500
When I say end-to-end
fashion, like in some way

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:22.160
to determine these way like--
to determine our compressor

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:24.890
algorithms or the exact
weights depending on,

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:26.360
like, the final
value of the rate,

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:30.650
distortion somehow figure
that out backwards.

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:34.550
And if you know ML, you know
what I mean by really end

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:35.330
to end, right?

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:38.030
So the whole premise
of ML is like you

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:43.340
try to do this end-to-end
learning for whatever you

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:46.260
are trying to optimize.

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:48.450
And so these are
basically, I would say,

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:53.550
the two main motivation and
ideas for learned compression.

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:57.210
Like learned compression just
means using machine learning.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.540
And it goes beyond all these
traditional compressors

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:03.750
by going beyond, first of all,
linear-to-nonlinear transform

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:05.940
and then also doing this
end-to-end training.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:08.980
And there has been,
like, a ton of work,

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:10.770
I would say, in just past.

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:14.640
I would say the one
of the early papers

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:18.450
by Toderici in CVPR15
and Theis in ICLR17--

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.630
like those were the first
papers which started

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:23.100
using ML for compression.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:26.562
But since then, there have been
like lots and lots of work.

00:14:26.562 --> 00:14:28.020
Basically, it's a
really hot field.

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.570
A lot of people
are contributing.

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:31.320
And hopefully, by the
end of this lecture,

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:34.320
I can convince you that it's
a really important topic

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.760
to learn if you want to--

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:37.590
if you want to
continue in compression

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:39.240
and keep contributing.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:42.810
At least you should know about
the techniques and ideas.

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:45.420
It's also very interesting
because there is a whole CLIC

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:45.960
Challenge.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:48.335
It's called Challenge on
Learned Image Compression, which

00:14:48.335 --> 00:14:51.620
is I think in its fourth
or fifth iteration now.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:53.840
And this is happening at
Data Compression Conference.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:56.132
And this is a really active
channel, a lot of industry,

00:14:56.132 --> 00:14:57.650
a lot of people,
a lot of academia

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:00.590
participate in this to come
up with the best compressors

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:02.900
for images and videos right?

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:06.560
So if-- after this class,
I hope you should also

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:08.540
be prepared if you
would like to take

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:11.590
participate in
challenges like this.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:13.020
OK, so lots of--

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:14.520
another point which
I want to make--

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:17.750
like lots and lots of
interesting works and ideas

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:21.450
will only focus on the basic
ideas like the fundamentals.

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:23.550
And then you can go
on and in future,

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:27.000
read whichever of these papers
you want to build on them.

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:31.720
Cool.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:33.290
Everyone with me?

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:37.840
[INAUDIBLE] OK, so before I go
into learned image compression,

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:41.320
since we decided we'll
not assume any background,

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:43.360
next few slides might
be boring for people

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:45.310
who are already aware about ML.

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:48.100
But if you don't know
anything about ML,

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:50.320
just hear through the
next five minutes.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.090
And hopefully, you
should have everything

00:15:52.090 --> 00:15:53.680
which you need to
know or at least

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:56.770
appreciating today's lecture
with regards to compression.

00:15:56.770 --> 00:16:00.320
But if you already know
it, don't worry about it.

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:04.660
So this is really 101,
thousand feet review, right?

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:07.180
There are lots and lots of
interesting classes which

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:09.940
you can take, which will
teach you in much more depth,

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:12.610
and I'll encourage
you guys to do that.

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:15.760
But there are three main
components to any ML pipeline,

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:16.780
really.

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:18.640
The first is data, right?

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:20.980
So you try to figure out--

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:24.190
you try to optimize some
problem based on the data which

00:16:24.190 --> 00:16:25.570
you have been given.

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:27.850
The second is some
nonlinear model

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.350
and differentiable
architectures,

00:16:29.350 --> 00:16:33.910
which is this f of x, which
is a predictor basically

00:16:33.910 --> 00:16:34.700
on your data.

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:38.270
So you can think of
x as your data inputs

00:16:38.270 --> 00:16:39.530
and y as your labels.

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:40.880
Let's look at some examples.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:42.920
For example, your
x can be images.

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:45.350
Y can be whether it is
a dog or cat or not.

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:48.800
So y can be a binary
label per image saying,

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:53.030
OK, whether there is a dog
in this image or not, right?

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:57.890
So your nonlinear model
or your function f

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:00.560
is basically trying to learn.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:04.089
It's trying to predict
whether there is a cat or not

00:17:04.089 --> 00:17:05.200
in your image.

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:08.349
So it's a function of
the input data x image.

00:17:08.349 --> 00:17:12.400
And typically, it is a
parameterizable model.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:14.950
What that means is it
typically has-- it's defined

00:17:14.950 --> 00:17:16.750
based on some parameters theta.

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:19.900
The standard notation for
this is something like this.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:21.040
All that means is like--

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:23.890
OK, if you recall a
rate-distortion water-filling

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:25.869
thing, water-filling
formula, there

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:30.820
also we had a theta, which was
a parameter to parameterize

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:31.960
a rate-distortion curve.

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:33.550
Think of theta in the same way.

00:17:33.550 --> 00:17:37.390
Basically, your function f just
depends on parameter theta.

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:40.471
And it takes input x, OK?

00:17:40.471 --> 00:17:42.460
The output of this is y hat.

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:46.050
Now, your y hat can be--

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:50.070
it can be involved, but
for now, think of it as--

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:52.800
what your model
thinks is the label

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:54.840
for this particular image, OK?

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:58.560
So maybe based on
whatever model I have,

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:01.560
when I input a particular
image, it thinks it's a cat.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:03.120
And it was indeed a cat.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:07.110
Or maybe it says it's dog, even
though the input image is cat.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:09.750
So it's figuring out
based on this whatever

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:12.180
function f on data
and parameters

00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:15.060
to get this prediction y hat.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:18.990
And this is really the second
main component of our pipeline.

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:21.600
And the final main one
is the loss function.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.000
So you need to
determine like, OK,

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:27.450
so if my model predicts some
cat or dog, whether it's correct

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:28.470
or not, right?

00:18:28.470 --> 00:18:30.900
So if given an image,
my model predicts cat,

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:35.370
one of the loss functions could
really be just a 1 hat, right?

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.580
So you can just decide
if it's an indicator, so

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:42.990
if the predicted value is
cat, whether it is indeed cat

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:46.770
or not, given the
original input label y.

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:48.780
OK, so very, very
high level overview.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:52.410
Mostly all of you are already
aware of this in today's world

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:57.460
and age, but start
from here, OK?

00:18:57.460 --> 00:19:02.920
And then the way this
ML pipeline really

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:05.320
works is your f can
be anything, right?

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:08.290
It's just a nonlinear
differentiable architecture.

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:10.270
It could be thousand
different things.

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:15.010
But the basic idea is you use
backpropagation or gradients

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:18.490
to actually learn the
model parameters theta.

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:20.410
And that's how you--
that's what's called

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:22.480
training or learning, right?

00:19:22.480 --> 00:19:24.850
And that happens based
on whatever given

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:28.200
data you have like x and y.

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:30.420
The way this
progresses is by taking

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:34.500
gradients of your loss function
with respect to theta, OK?

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:36.690
So here, all I
have done is like--

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:38.400
remember our loss was just--

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:40.920
it tells you whether you were
correct or not with respect

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:44.640
to the label, which
was given to you based

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:45.840
on the predicted label.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:48.630
Here, I've just replaced
my predicted label

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:53.580
by f, X of theta, which is
what that prediction really is.

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:55.470
And what you want
to do is you want

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:57.690
to minimize this loss function.

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:59.640
So this is an
optimization problem.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:02.670
You want to figure out
parameters theta, which

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:04.380
basically minimizes your loss.

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:06.910
So you want to train
your model or parameters

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:10.230
so that your overall
objective function reduces,

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:13.200
which is this loss function.

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:15.900
And the way you reduce this-- so
this is a general optimization

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:16.400
problem.

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:20.340
So if you know about
convex optimization

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:21.780
and non-convex
optimization, this

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:25.200
is, like, maybe easy to solve,
maybe hard to solve typically

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:27.690
in our modern day machine
learning and world.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:31.320
It's typically a non-convex
optimization problem,

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:34.620
and so it's really hard to
solve this problem exactly.

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:37.530
But what you can do is you can
do a vanilla gradient descent.

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:40.650
What that means is that you
update the parameters theta

00:20:40.650 --> 00:20:44.310
at each time step by taking a
gradient with respect to theta

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:47.010
of this loss function.

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:47.510
OK?

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:51.080
And you keep doing
this repeatedly,

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:53.510
and that basically updates
your parameter theta

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:59.310
and gets it to a point so as
this optimization function

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:00.870
is really--

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:03.240
this objective function
is really optimized.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:06.300
So you are minimizing the
loss, or you are basically

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:10.570
finding a model f, which is
giving you the correct labels

00:21:10.570 --> 00:21:11.070
y.

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:15.640
OK?

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:19.570
So if you are seeing
this for the first time,

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:20.560
are you with me?

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:21.820
Is there any questions?

00:21:21.820 --> 00:21:24.860
Are there any questions?

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:27.373
So this is, like, the basic
training architecture like what

00:21:27.373 --> 00:21:28.540
you are really trying to do.

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:30.280
All you are trying
to do is you are

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:33.280
trying to find a model, which
takes an input from data

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:36.670
x, outputs a
prediction label y hat.

00:21:36.670 --> 00:21:39.790
The way you are trying to find--
you are trying to find out

00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:44.300
this model is instead of, OK,
maybe doing DFT, DCT, blah,

00:21:44.300 --> 00:21:46.420
blah, blah, some
hand-driven model,

00:21:46.420 --> 00:21:49.840
you are trying to
learn this model using

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:51.130
the data which you have.

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:54.230
The way you learn it is you need
to define this loss function,

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:55.870
which basically
tells you difference

00:21:55.870 --> 00:21:57.430
between the actual labels.

00:21:57.430 --> 00:21:59.980
So is it actually cat
or not versus predicted

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:02.530
labels, which is what
your model is saying.

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:06.280
And then you backpropagate it
or optimize this loss function

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:06.920
using--

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:10.670
by passing gradients
through the network.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:14.050
And that updates your
model parameter theta

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:16.720
eventually leading in reduction
of this loss function.

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:25.070
OK, and so again, like
there are a lot of--

00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:27.110
I'm trying to summarize
this in five minutes

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:29.810
like a lot of details and like--

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:31.940
yeah, I'm not sure if
it's helpful or not,

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:34.880
but there are a lot
of classes which

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:36.140
goes into much more details.

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:37.400
Do check it.

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:39.260
But this is
basically the summary

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:42.200
at the very least which you need
to understand and appreciate

00:22:42.200 --> 00:22:45.150
to understand today's class.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.570
So the first thing
is, in ML, you

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:49.820
use data to learn
the model parameters

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:51.290
for optimizing a loss.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:54.020
So these are the three
things which we wanted to--

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:57.110
the three main components
which are there in your machine

00:22:57.110 --> 00:22:58.610
learning pipeline.

00:22:58.610 --> 00:23:02.030
You do this by taking
gradients of your loss function

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:03.350
with respect to theta.

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:05.840
And your losses now--

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:07.880
it depends on the theta
because your prediction

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:10.270
depends on the theta.

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:10.780
OK?

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:12.760
And the main
advantage it provides

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:15.730
over this non-learned
models is really,

00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:20.920
it allows optimizing essentially
any objective function loss

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:25.330
as long as your function
is differentiable, OK?

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:28.660
What that means is so as to
be able to do these gradients,

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:31.750
you should be able
to take this--

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:35.200
you should be able-- your
loss should be differentiable.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:37.690
And that typically means
like if your f consists

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:40.150
of multiple layers, you should
be able to differentiate

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:41.380
through them.

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:43.390
It's basic application
of chain rule.

00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:47.350
And again, I know like, go
check out this if you are not

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:48.565
following it completely.

00:23:48.565 --> 00:23:50.440
If you don't know about
it, trust me on this.

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:53.110
You need the layers to
be differentiable so as

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:55.450
to be able to do any learning.

00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:58.360
What that means for us is
that it doesn't play well

00:23:58.360 --> 00:23:59.980
with discrete
distributions, right?

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:02.350
So our whole
lossless compression

00:24:02.350 --> 00:24:05.770
has worked with some
discrete distributions.

00:24:05.770 --> 00:24:08.710
And so you can't just
like go and backpropagate

00:24:08.710 --> 00:24:10.810
or fit in a standard
machine learning model

00:24:10.810 --> 00:24:12.970
and get a learned
compressor because you

00:24:12.970 --> 00:24:15.430
have to somehow deal with
the discreteness part

00:24:15.430 --> 00:24:18.910
of the entropy coder.

00:24:18.910 --> 00:24:23.560
The second main advantage is
that it allows you stacking

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:25.240
of these nonlinear layers.

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:29.620
So your model f need not
be linear anymore, OK?

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:31.120
What is a linear model?

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:35.320
It's basically multiplication--
it's just a matrix, right,

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:37.390
a matrix multiplication.

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:41.020
And if you think about it like
stacking of linear layers--

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:43.960
so going back to your
linear algebra again.

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:46.960
Stacking of linear layers
is basically not so powerful

00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:49.240
because if you were
multiplying three, or four,

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:51.130
or whatever number
of matrices together,

00:24:51.130 --> 00:24:54.550
you could just represent this
as a single matrix, right?

00:24:54.550 --> 00:24:59.050
So if you had ABC,
whatever, stacked matrix,

00:24:59.050 --> 00:25:00.760
it still gives you--
you can represent it

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:01.690
as a single matrix.

00:25:01.690 --> 00:25:05.020
So it's not really giving
you any more expressivity

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:08.590
in your model, whereas if
you have nonlinear layer,

00:25:08.590 --> 00:25:11.350
and then you stack them,
then this is no longer true.

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:14.170
And that's really the second
main benefit of this ML.

00:25:14.170 --> 00:25:17.200
That's why if you have ever
heard about deep neural nets,

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:18.850
and like let's--

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:21.903
we went from billion parameters
to million parameters

00:25:21.903 --> 00:25:24.070
to billion parameters to
hundred million parameters.

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:26.710
And the way we do it is keep
on increasing the depth,

00:25:26.710 --> 00:25:27.880
and we need more data.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:31.870
All of these are very punchlines
you keep hearing outside.

00:25:31.870 --> 00:25:36.130
All of them have basically
got to do with this idea that

00:25:36.130 --> 00:25:40.390
in machine learning-- or sorry,
in-- yeah, in linear case,

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:42.880
there is no advantage of,
like, stacking linear layers,

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:45.700
but in a nonlinear case, there
is a lot of advantage of, like,

00:25:45.700 --> 00:25:48.620
stacking linear layers--

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:50.140
oh, sorry, nonlinear layers.

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:53.600
Cool.

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:57.390
Any questions?

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:01.430
So this is just like an overview
of a standard ML pipeline.

00:26:01.430 --> 00:26:03.380
Finally, this is
the last thing which

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:04.820
we need for today's lecture.

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:07.130
Again, you don't
have to worry if you

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:09.950
haven't seen this before.

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:12.020
But there is another
architecture called

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:14.000
autoencoder architecture.

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:16.760
And the idea really is
that you have some input x.

00:26:16.760 --> 00:26:19.070
So this is the same input, OK?

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:21.020
This whole thing
you can think of it

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:23.450
as your model like the-- sorry.

00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:25.970
Yeah, so I was saying so
now, the last thing which

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:29.000
you need to know to appreciate
this lecture from ML

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:32.030
is this idea from
autoencoder architecture.

00:26:32.030 --> 00:26:34.610
Just really so now
you have some input x.

00:26:34.610 --> 00:26:37.310
You can think of this
encoder/decoder pair

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:38.420
as your model.

00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:42.980
And that outputs your
y or x dash here, which

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:44.810
is the reconstructed input.

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:47.510
OK, so the main idea
here really is that--

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:50.330
so in our typical
pipeline, we also

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:52.580
needed the true labels,
so whether it's a cat

00:26:52.580 --> 00:26:54.530
or not, right?

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:57.300
But the idea of this
autoencoder architecture

00:26:57.300 --> 00:27:00.270
really is you try to
recover an output which

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:02.810
is similar as the input.

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:05.720
OK, and then your loss
function can be determined such

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:10.240
that you basically want your
x dash to be similar to x.

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:12.490
So your loss function
can be over x and x dash.

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:14.680
So you can think you
start with an image.

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:16.837
You make it go through
some architecture, which

00:27:16.837 --> 00:27:17.920
we'll come to in a second.

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:20.740
This is your f, X of,
in this case, theta phi,

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:22.300
which are the parameters.

00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:24.550
And then the output
is just x dash.

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:27.700
And then your loss function
will be over some x and x dash.

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:29.350
So you start with an image.

00:27:29.350 --> 00:27:31.930
You recover an image, right?

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:35.170
The way you do it is
that your model actually

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:37.690
has a very specific
architecture where you first

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:42.220
have an encoder, which reduces
the dimension of your input

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:45.610
to something small,
this bottleneck z.

00:27:45.610 --> 00:27:50.380
And then using that bottleneck
z, you try to decompress it.

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:52.060
So you have
something which tries

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:53.770
to increase the
number of parameters

00:27:53.770 --> 00:27:57.070
and bring it back
to the image, OK?

00:27:57.070 --> 00:27:59.590
Does this architecture
remind you of something?

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:05.650
They hint us like this.

00:28:05.650 --> 00:28:07.745
Look at the words
with respect to z.

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:14.370
OK, come on.

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:18.480
Yeah, so this is exactly
in some-- not exactly,

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:22.150
but this is in some sense very
similar to compression problem,

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:22.650
right?

00:28:22.650 --> 00:28:26.490
So what you are trying to do
is you are forcing your network

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:29.250
to learn an encoder
and a decoder pair--

00:28:29.250 --> 00:28:31.950
so these parameters
phi and theta--

00:28:31.950 --> 00:28:35.970
such that whatever this
low-dimensional bottleneck is,

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:39.060
which is typically called
the latent variables,

00:28:39.060 --> 00:28:42.780
you are able to recover an
image from that or x dash

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:46.360
from that, which looks very
similar to the original input.

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:49.490
This is exactly what we want
in some sense in compression,

00:28:49.490 --> 00:28:49.990
right?

00:28:49.990 --> 00:28:53.740
Like what JPEG does is you
start with that input images, x.

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:57.400
You want some
low-dimensional bitstream

00:28:57.400 --> 00:29:00.040
or not-- actually, there is no
low dimensional, no continuous

00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:00.730
thing there.

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:04.000
But you basically want
to represent this image

00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:07.120
in terms of as few number
of bits as possible, which

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:08.350
is, like, your bottleneck.

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:11.020
And then you start with that
bottleneck like few bits

00:29:11.020 --> 00:29:13.240
and try to recover back
an image, which looks very

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:16.310
similar to the original one.

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:18.860
OK, so this is a very
popular architecture called

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:20.210
autoencoder architecture.

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:23.130
And again, there are hundred--

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:26.040
tens of fundamentals variants
of it and then hundreds

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:29.940
of more variants on top of
each of these tens of variants.

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:32.370
So it's a very, very
popular architecture.

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:34.080
But the idea really
is you are trying

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:36.690
to learn parameters,
such that you

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:40.200
compress so as to say whatever
original input you start with.

00:29:40.200 --> 00:29:43.080
And then you
decompress to get back

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:44.310
something which is original.

00:29:44.310 --> 00:29:46.890
And you do try to learn this
in an end-to-end fashion.

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:49.580
You determine a loss
over this x and x dash.

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:53.800
Cool.

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:57.002
Everyone with me?

00:29:57.002 --> 00:29:58.980
Huh?

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:00.810
So now, let's get into our--

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:04.230
again, the image compression
problem, so same thing

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:07.680
what we looked at, but now
in words and terminologies

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:10.920
which we understand and how we
have looked into this problem.

00:30:10.920 --> 00:30:14.940
So let's say you have some
target image like these cats.

00:30:14.940 --> 00:30:17.190
What do you want to do
is you want to encode it.

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:19.230
That's your encoder
to some bitstream.

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:20.790
And then using
this bitstream, you

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:23.400
want to decode it and get
some target reconstruction

00:30:23.400 --> 00:30:24.660
like I hat.

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:27.930
And the goal really is to do
this R-D optimization, right?

00:30:27.930 --> 00:30:30.000
So you want to
minimize the distortion

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:33.000
between original image and
the reconstructed image

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:36.660
given some constraint on
the rate, which is just

00:30:36.660 --> 00:30:40.020
the length of this bit should
be less than or equals to B. OK,

00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:42.030
so this is what you
really want to achieve

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:44.460
at the end of the day.

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:47.630
And so in traditional
image codecs,

00:30:47.630 --> 00:30:50.130
essentially, the encoding was
working in three steps, right?

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:51.990
So we first did DCT.

00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:54.750
And earlier, we call this
symbol something else,

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:56.550
but for this talk,
let's call it z.

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:59.610
And the reason is we're
going to correlate it back

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:00.780
to these latent variables.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:03.060
It's a very commonly
used symbol z for it.

00:31:03.060 --> 00:31:05.820
So first step was
DCT to decorrelate.

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:08.310
The second step is
quantization, right?

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:10.590
So this is where the
loss gets introduced

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:12.570
like loss of precision.

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:15.150
And then you would do
some arithmetic encoding

00:31:15.150 --> 00:31:18.330
on these discrete symbols, let's
say, or your favorite lossless

00:31:18.330 --> 00:31:20.220
entropy coder for that purpose.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:23.310
For this talk, let's assume
you do arithmetic encoding.

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:26.190
The idea you do arithmetic
encoding is now,

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:28.170
you can assume that
your length of bits

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:31.020
is approximately equal
to log 1 over P of z hat.

00:31:31.020 --> 00:31:34.170
And again, we have looked into
this through many homeworks

00:31:34.170 --> 00:31:34.840
and classes.

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:38.910
So I am hopeful now this
idea is really, deep down,

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:40.830
you guys understand it.

00:31:40.830 --> 00:31:41.730
OK.

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:44.250
And so then what happens
on the decoder side--

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:45.420
you start with this bits.

00:31:45.420 --> 00:31:48.030
You do decoding, which
basically gives you

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:50.910
these discretized
latent variables z hat.

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:54.000
And now you do directly
inverse DCT transform, right?

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:56.400
So this was where the loss
was introduced, right?

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:59.680
IDCT and DCT were completely
reversible transform.

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:02.110
And this gives you this
target reconstruction.

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:06.630
So now the question
is, like, how

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:08.430
can you improve this, right?

00:32:08.430 --> 00:32:11.550
So the first idea was,
OK, we don't really

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:14.130
need these linear
transforms, right?

00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:17.160
Like here, we were
doing DCT and IDCT.

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:22.200
And we justified it saying that,
OK, DCT [AUDIO OUT] similar way

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:25.020
as we would expect in
natural signals, right?

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:27.900
But after that, we had some
different quantization method,

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:29.700
and so on and so forth.

00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:31.860
We are not limited to
the linear transform.

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:33.810
So let's try to learn
something general.

00:32:33.810 --> 00:32:36.900
Instead of doing
DCT and IDCT, let's

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:40.020
try to learn some of these--

00:32:40.020 --> 00:32:45.120
let's try to learn nonlinear
transforms for doing this image

00:32:45.120 --> 00:32:46.500
compression, right?

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:49.440
And that basically just
means replacing so this--

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:53.310
replacing this DCT
and IDCT with deep,

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:56.400
let's say, feedforward networks
or any network for that sort.

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:58.410
Don't worry about the
architecture for most part

00:32:58.410 --> 00:32:59.970
of this talk.

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:02.140
We are not going to get
into much of the ML details,

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:06.160
but rather stick to the
fundamental concepts, OK?

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:08.650
And the idea really
is that if you

00:33:08.650 --> 00:33:10.840
use these deep
feedforward networks,

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:13.600
we should still get this
energy segregation, right?

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:16.960
Like otherwise, there is no
benefit of really doing that.

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:19.220
And how do you
ensure you do that?

00:33:19.220 --> 00:33:22.660
So you need to determine some
loss function really, right?

00:33:22.660 --> 00:33:24.790
Like so how do you
try to figure out what

00:33:24.790 --> 00:33:26.350
these parameters should be?

00:33:26.350 --> 00:33:31.020
In an ML pipeline, we need to
determine some loss function.

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:35.920
So one obvious loss
function is like, which

00:33:35.920 --> 00:33:38.733
is what we started out to do
is the rate-distortion loss

00:33:38.733 --> 00:33:39.400
function, right?

00:33:39.400 --> 00:33:42.460
So what we want to do is
we want to minimize rate.

00:33:42.460 --> 00:33:44.830
And so this is just the
Lagrangian formulation.

00:33:44.830 --> 00:33:47.950
So lambda is just a
positive constant.

00:33:47.950 --> 00:33:50.320
So when you are trying to
optimize two variables,

00:33:50.320 --> 00:33:53.860
you can use this lambda to
trade off rate with distortion.

00:33:53.860 --> 00:33:55.630
So one thing you
want to minimize

00:33:55.630 --> 00:33:58.150
is this distortion
between I and I hat.

00:33:58.150 --> 00:33:59.830
The other thing you
want to minimize

00:33:59.830 --> 00:34:04.250
is this length of bitstream
in the middle right.

00:34:04.250 --> 00:34:06.680
OK?

00:34:06.680 --> 00:34:10.100
But we know that we can
approximate the bitstream

00:34:10.100 --> 00:34:15.690
length by this log 1
over P of z hat, OK?

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:18.820
So this is one very
obvious loss function,

00:34:18.820 --> 00:34:22.380
which we can use to train
this network back using

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:24.659
backpropagating and
using gradients.

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:29.840
OK?

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:35.510
So one way to think about this
is instead of directly doing

00:34:35.510 --> 00:34:38.360
arithmetic coding, what
we need in our pipeline

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:40.310
is this probability
model really, right?

00:34:40.310 --> 00:34:41.840
So you need to
determine-- you need

00:34:41.840 --> 00:34:45.850
to know what this P of
z hat is so as to be

00:34:45.850 --> 00:34:47.755
able to train this network.

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:56.080
OK, so what-- anyone
wants to point out what

00:34:56.080 --> 00:34:58.750
is an issue with this pipeline?

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:00.370
So we have defined this.

00:35:00.370 --> 00:35:03.010
If I just backpropagate
on this loss function,

00:35:03.010 --> 00:35:05.530
take gradients with respect
to this loss functions,

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:07.360
can we just learn the END.

00:35:19.020 --> 00:35:23.370
Anyone who's familiar with ML
or what we just talked about?

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:28.230
So for learning, what
do you really need?

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:32.460
You need your objective function
to be differentiable, right?

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:34.860
Is this whole pipeline
differentiable?

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:40.640
Can I-- when I back-- when
I compute the gradients

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:42.590
with respect to my
parameters, I will

00:35:42.590 --> 00:35:45.850
have to compute it
all the way backwards.

00:35:49.180 --> 00:35:50.785
Can somebody point some issues?

00:35:56.660 --> 00:35:58.310
Yeah?

00:35:58.310 --> 00:36:00.920
Getting the probability
distribution involves

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:03.290
quantizing first,
and quantizing isn't

00:36:03.290 --> 00:36:04.430
going to be differentiable.

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:06.860
Yeah, OK, so as
one of the students

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:10.317
answered, getting the
probability distribution,

00:36:10.317 --> 00:36:11.900
we first have to get
the z hat, right?

00:36:11.900 --> 00:36:14.060
There is a quantization
step in the middle.

00:36:14.060 --> 00:36:16.430
But I can't really take--

00:36:16.430 --> 00:36:18.800
I can't do differentiation
across the quantization

00:36:18.800 --> 00:36:20.480
because that's a
discrete variable.

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:23.900
So discrete operation--
I can't really do that.

00:36:23.900 --> 00:36:25.460
That's correct.

00:36:25.460 --> 00:36:26.390
Anything else?

00:36:26.390 --> 00:36:27.980
Is there any other
operation which

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:31.425
you think you can't
backpropagate or take gradients

00:36:31.425 --> 00:36:31.925
across?

00:36:36.030 --> 00:36:39.450
So let's say instead
of this z hat, I had z.

00:36:39.450 --> 00:36:42.480
For a second, assume there
was no quantization step.

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:46.110
I just had log 1 over--

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:46.860
actually, no.

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:50.870
Let's say you had
quantization step.

00:36:50.870 --> 00:36:53.260
So the other issue
is you also have

00:36:53.260 --> 00:36:57.610
to take gradients across the
probability model, right?

00:36:57.610 --> 00:37:01.090
So one issue is obviously
quantization as the student

00:37:01.090 --> 00:37:01.900
pointed out, right?

00:37:01.900 --> 00:37:03.790
Like how do you-- how
do you take gradients

00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:05.260
across sort of this layer?

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:06.970
But the other issue
is also like, how

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:08.620
do you take gradients
across your log 1

00:37:08.620 --> 00:37:09.940
over probability of z hat?

00:37:09.940 --> 00:37:14.620
This is a discrete
[AUDIO OUT] right?

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:17.830
So this is like--
this is what you are--

00:37:17.830 --> 00:37:21.700
this is what your-- what you
were entropy coding losslessly.

00:37:21.700 --> 00:37:25.060
So both of these
layers-- actually, you

00:37:25.060 --> 00:37:26.770
can backpropagate through it.

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:31.330
So that basically stops you
from learning end to end.

00:37:31.330 --> 00:37:35.140
And so there are some
simple workarounds.

00:37:35.140 --> 00:37:37.210
Let's look at both of them.

00:37:37.210 --> 00:37:40.360
So for quantizer, let's say
our quantizer is just simple

00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:41.193
rounding for now.

00:37:41.193 --> 00:37:42.610
So that's like a
scalar quantizer.

00:37:42.610 --> 00:37:46.220
I just round it to
nearest integer.

00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:50.420
So the way people work on it
is like during training, you

00:37:50.420 --> 00:37:52.490
model the quantizer
as if you are adding

00:37:52.490 --> 00:37:54.485
some independent noise,
which is like uniform

00:37:54.485 --> 00:37:57.200
in minus 0.5 to 0.5, OK?

00:37:57.200 --> 00:37:59.420
So basically, your
2.3 is going to 2.

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:01.100
3.7 is going to 4.

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:06.140
Let me just model it as adding a
noise, which is plus minus 0.5.

00:38:06.140 --> 00:38:10.330
And now this operation
becomes differentiable, right?

00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:13.270
What that basically
means is when

00:38:13.270 --> 00:38:16.750
you are differentiating through
this, you straight away--

00:38:16.750 --> 00:38:20.500
like your d of z of hat
is going to be d of z.

00:38:20.500 --> 00:38:23.740
So you have something called
a straight-through estimator.

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:27.365
So you behave as if this
quantization layer didn't even

00:38:27.365 --> 00:38:27.865
exist.

00:38:30.370 --> 00:38:31.030
OK?

00:38:31.030 --> 00:38:34.000
While taking forward pass,
while you are actually

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:38.020
calculating the parameters, you
model it as by adding noise.

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:41.290
But while taking gradients
and updating your parameters,

00:38:41.290 --> 00:38:43.750
you assume you are not--
you're not doing anything.

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:45.970
And again, this is an--
this is an assumption.

00:38:45.970 --> 00:38:48.070
Like there is no proof to it.

00:38:48.070 --> 00:38:49.690
This is just an assumption.

00:38:49.690 --> 00:38:52.300
And it seems to work
extremely good empirically.

00:38:52.300 --> 00:38:53.950
That's what has been observed.

00:38:53.950 --> 00:38:55.420
And this is, like,
actually trick

00:38:55.420 --> 00:38:58.180
number one for dealing
with, I guess, quantization

00:38:58.180 --> 00:39:01.180
steps anywhere or whenever you
deal with anything discrete.

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:04.660
You model it as some
continuous variable

00:39:04.660 --> 00:39:06.430
z plus some noise added.

00:39:06.430 --> 00:39:08.440
And in terms of
gradients, you just

00:39:08.440 --> 00:39:10.420
do a straight-through
estimator, which

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:13.120
just means you assume
that layer doesn't exist.

00:39:13.120 --> 00:39:16.430
It's just identity.

00:39:16.430 --> 00:39:18.330
OK.

00:39:18.330 --> 00:39:21.790
