So basically here, this is
what the actual my P of z hat

01:04:02.000 --> 01:04:03.980
looks like.

01:04:03.980 --> 01:04:06.110
OK, and it roughly
looks like what we might

01:04:06.110 --> 01:04:07.850
have hoped to be Gaussian.

01:04:07.850 --> 01:04:09.650
The second thing
which you see is

01:04:09.650 --> 01:04:15.380
that if you look at the final
rate and estimated rate,

01:04:15.380 --> 01:04:17.180
they are very close.

01:04:17.180 --> 01:04:19.280
So final rate is
when you actually

01:04:19.280 --> 01:04:22.040
apply an entropy encoder or
whatever discrete symbols

01:04:22.040 --> 01:04:22.880
you got.

01:04:22.880 --> 01:04:26.630
Your estimated rate is just
the log 1 over P of z hat,

01:04:26.630 --> 01:04:29.720
where we had an assumed
probability model, right?

01:04:29.720 --> 01:04:31.400
So this is a good sight.

01:04:31.400 --> 01:04:34.250
It basically says, again,
our arithmetic encoder

01:04:34.250 --> 01:04:35.630
is doing the right job.

01:04:35.630 --> 01:04:38.120
Like it's roughly
compressing things too.

01:04:38.120 --> 01:04:41.660
Actually, you can be more--

01:04:41.660 --> 01:04:43.550
you can be more
rigorous about this.

01:04:43.550 --> 01:04:46.220
We know that arithmetic
entropy coder stays

01:04:46.220 --> 01:04:47.960
within 2 bits of overhead.

01:04:47.960 --> 01:04:50.630
And if you look at this final
rate and estimated rate,

01:04:50.630 --> 01:04:53.450
you will see, OK, they are
roughly-- they are always

01:04:53.450 --> 01:04:56.180
almost within 2 bits, right?

01:04:56.180 --> 01:05:00.260
So OK, our arithmetic
encoder did the correct job.

01:05:00.260 --> 01:05:04.790
And so at this point,
OK, we can actually

01:05:04.790 --> 01:05:07.640
look at the reconstructed
images and what

01:05:07.640 --> 01:05:08.990
the original images are.

01:05:08.990 --> 01:05:10.880
So on the left here,
what we are showing

01:05:10.880 --> 01:05:14.450
is the original image
x, and on the right

01:05:14.450 --> 01:05:19.190
is the reconstructed image x hat
using this learned compressor,

01:05:19.190 --> 01:05:19.700
OK?

01:05:19.700 --> 01:05:22.880
And so for example here
just using 89 bits,

01:05:22.880 --> 01:05:27.190
you start with this 5, and
you get this reconstruction 5.

01:05:27.190 --> 01:05:29.590
And so overall, this
compressor doesn't look back.

01:05:29.590 --> 01:05:32.840
We literally
[AUDIO OUT] right now.

01:05:32.840 --> 01:05:36.010
And it's doing
somewhat [AUDIO OUT]

01:05:36.010 --> 01:05:40.810
These are, again, 28 by 28, so
quite 900 dimensional images.

01:05:40.810 --> 01:05:45.910
Like bits and [AUDIO OUT]
black and whites are just 28

01:05:45.910 --> 01:05:49.200
by 28, which is
roughly like 900 bits,

01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:52.810
[? something ?] like
approximately 90 bits.

01:05:52.810 --> 01:05:54.910
We have come up with
a learned compressor,

01:05:54.910 --> 01:05:58.310
which reconstructs [AUDIO OUT]
most like the original.

01:05:58.310 --> 01:05:59.480
So pretty cool, right?

01:05:59.480 --> 01:06:03.050
And we didn't have to
worry about figuring out

01:06:03.050 --> 01:06:06.350
all the designing, all
the hand-tuned parameters

01:06:06.350 --> 01:06:07.490
for this particular one.

01:06:07.490 --> 01:06:09.273
We figured out the
exact transform.

01:06:09.273 --> 01:06:10.940
You didn't have to
know about, oh, there

01:06:10.940 --> 01:06:13.730
exists something called
DCT, and so on and so forth

01:06:13.730 --> 01:06:16.100
if you had this
technology and everything.

01:06:16.100 --> 01:06:19.130
Before, you would have
probably rediscovered DCT

01:06:19.130 --> 01:06:21.240
using something like this.

01:06:21.240 --> 01:06:22.760
So that's pretty cool.

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:28.880
So the next thing which I just
want to show you-- oh, yeah.

01:06:28.880 --> 01:06:30.050
So here is the plot.

01:06:30.050 --> 01:06:30.770
OK.

01:06:30.770 --> 01:06:32.570
I thought I had it above.

01:06:32.570 --> 01:06:33.380
Sorry.

01:06:33.380 --> 01:06:34.630
OK.

01:06:34.630 --> 01:06:38.560
So this is again, the same
plot, which we looked back

01:06:38.560 --> 01:06:43.980
in the lecture,
right, so this thing.

01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:46.740
But just to show you
guys again, here, ,

01:06:46.740 --> 01:06:51.660
the blue curve is the
standard normal PDF, OK?

01:06:51.660 --> 01:06:55.148
The red is basically
the quantized PDF value.

01:06:55.148 --> 01:06:56.940
So this is, like, what
your model suggests.

01:06:56.940 --> 01:07:03.810
So this is just the CDF of z hat
plus 0.5 and z hat minus 0.5,

01:07:03.810 --> 01:07:04.710
OK?

01:07:04.710 --> 01:07:08.700
So the red points are same
as the blue dots here.

01:07:08.700 --> 01:07:11.280
This is what we expect
our model to be.

01:07:11.280 --> 01:07:15.000
And green is actually the
empirical probabilities.

01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:19.200
So remember this z hat table,
which I was showing you

01:07:19.200 --> 01:07:21.300
for different values of z hat.

01:07:21.300 --> 01:07:24.330
This is, like, exactly
these values, green one.

01:07:24.330 --> 01:07:27.330
So you can see the green
is not exactly same as red,

01:07:27.330 --> 01:07:30.840
but it's roughly following
the red architecture, right?

01:07:30.840 --> 01:07:32.130
And so it's roughly Gaussian.

01:07:32.130 --> 01:07:34.925
And there is a trade-off
between what distortion and all

01:07:34.925 --> 01:07:36.300
those things,
which will actually

01:07:36.300 --> 01:07:38.880
determine the empirical
distribution compared

01:07:38.880 --> 01:07:41.685
to actual.

01:07:41.685 --> 01:07:43.560
The final thing which
I want to show is this.

01:07:43.560 --> 01:07:45.750
Like if you increase
the lambda--

01:07:45.750 --> 01:07:46.870
OK, this is interesting.

01:07:46.870 --> 01:07:49.287
So what do you think happens
when you increase the lambda?

01:07:53.540 --> 01:07:56.060
So how do you actually do the
rate-distortion trade-off,

01:07:56.060 --> 01:07:56.560
right?

01:07:56.560 --> 01:07:58.100
So we have trained a compressor.

01:07:58.100 --> 01:08:00.200
Actually, this is
a one tricky point

01:08:00.200 --> 01:08:03.272
about the ML-based compressor,
so learned compressors.

01:08:03.272 --> 01:08:04.730
So we have trained
our compressors,

01:08:04.730 --> 01:08:07.250
which work really well for
a given value of lambda.

01:08:07.250 --> 01:08:10.100
It trades off rate and
distortion very well

01:08:10.100 --> 01:08:12.530
when I fixed what
should be my trade-off.

01:08:12.530 --> 01:08:16.130
But you can imagine if I
change my value of lambda,

01:08:16.130 --> 01:08:17.870
my learned rates
will be different.

01:08:17.870 --> 01:08:20.240
And they might value rate
more or distortion more,

01:08:20.240 --> 01:08:22.550
which is something a
knob, which we always want

01:08:22.550 --> 01:08:23.990
in lossy compression, right?

01:08:23.990 --> 01:08:25.880
So in our case, it's lambda.

01:08:25.880 --> 01:08:29.420
So let's say here, earlier the
curves were with lambda 2,000.

01:08:29.420 --> 01:08:31.970
Now, let's say I
reduce lambda to 500.

01:08:31.970 --> 01:08:35.420
And I'm optimizing for rate
plus lambda times distortion.

01:08:35.420 --> 01:08:36.290
What do you expect?

01:08:36.290 --> 01:08:37.939
Do you expect your
reconstructions

01:08:37.939 --> 01:08:41.090
to be more lossy or less lossy?

01:08:41.090 --> 01:08:42.810
More lossy.

01:08:42.810 --> 01:08:44.026
OK, I hear more lossy.

01:08:44.026 --> 01:08:46.109
How many of you think it's
going to be more lossy?

01:08:49.560 --> 01:08:53.270
How many of you think it's
going to be less lossy?

01:08:53.270 --> 01:08:53.770
Cool.

01:08:53.770 --> 01:08:55.500
I think everyone
almost said more lossy,

01:08:55.500 --> 01:08:57.960
and some people are
probably not sure.

01:08:57.960 --> 01:08:58.950
Yes, exactly.

01:08:58.950 --> 01:09:01.229
It's going to be more lossy
because now you're putting

01:09:01.229 --> 01:09:02.790
less weight to distortion.

01:09:02.790 --> 01:09:04.103
You're reducing lambda.

01:09:04.103 --> 01:09:06.270
So in your loss function,
you're putting less weight

01:09:06.270 --> 01:09:06.990
to distortion.

01:09:06.990 --> 01:09:09.000
So you're in a
sense, saying, OK,

01:09:09.000 --> 01:09:12.180
I'm fine with giving me more
distortion, but reduce my rate.

01:09:12.180 --> 01:09:14.100
Maybe I'm in a really
network constrained

01:09:14.100 --> 01:09:15.960
thing, constrained environment.

01:09:15.960 --> 01:09:16.680
Reduce my rate.

01:09:16.680 --> 01:09:19.649
I'm OK with taking
more distortion.

01:09:19.649 --> 01:09:21.930
And so this exactly--

01:09:21.930 --> 01:09:23.490
oops, I had actually trained it.

01:09:23.490 --> 01:09:25.410
Now I'm retraining it.

01:09:25.410 --> 01:09:27.779
But this has gone to train,
and this is exactly what

01:09:27.779 --> 01:09:28.680
we are going to see.

01:09:28.680 --> 01:09:33.420
Violet trains-- let me just come
back here and talk a little bit

01:09:33.420 --> 01:09:37.702
about what are some of
the benefits and issues

01:09:37.702 --> 01:09:38.910
with this learned compressor.

01:09:38.910 --> 01:09:43.260
And then we'll come back,
and I'll show you that part.

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:46.080
So a couple of
advantages-- first,

01:09:46.080 --> 01:09:48.149
there is no hand-tuning
of parameters.

01:09:48.149 --> 01:09:50.250
You can learn the parameters.

01:09:50.250 --> 01:09:53.370
What that means is
you have adaptivity,

01:09:53.370 --> 01:09:55.620
which was not possible with
traditional codecs, right?

01:09:55.620 --> 01:09:58.470
Your traditional codecs
always had the same matrices,

01:09:58.470 --> 01:10:00.300
but now you can
have, for example,

01:10:00.300 --> 01:10:01.890
custom codec for each domain.

01:10:01.890 --> 01:10:04.680
You can have something for
cartoons, something for games,

01:10:04.680 --> 01:10:05.820
something for TV series.

01:10:05.820 --> 01:10:09.985
And you can figure out
different transform code values

01:10:09.985 --> 01:10:11.610
for each of them,
which performs better

01:10:11.610 --> 01:10:13.410
for that particular
kind of data.

01:10:13.410 --> 01:10:15.180
You can have something
content-aware

01:10:15.180 --> 01:10:16.200
or even task-aware.

01:10:16.200 --> 01:10:17.790
So you basically
get the flexibility

01:10:17.790 --> 01:10:20.373
because now you can train these
end-to-end learned compressors

01:10:20.373 --> 01:10:22.980
for a specific task.

01:10:22.980 --> 01:10:26.340
The second idea is you get
a better distortion model

01:10:26.340 --> 01:10:30.420
separation, which basically says
that here, my distortion could

01:10:30.420 --> 01:10:35.730
have been anything, right, as
long as it's differentiable.

01:10:35.730 --> 01:10:37.560
What that means is that--

01:10:37.560 --> 01:10:39.900
so if you recall,
long time back,

01:10:39.900 --> 01:10:41.160
we talked about human vision.

01:10:41.160 --> 01:10:43.560
And we said MSE is
not the right thing.

01:10:43.560 --> 01:10:44.610
Guess what?

01:10:44.610 --> 01:10:48.810
We train something right now,
which was optimized for L1.

01:10:48.810 --> 01:10:51.150
I could have easily
optimized for L2.

01:10:51.150 --> 01:10:53.480
But nobody is
stopping us at L2--

01:10:53.480 --> 01:10:54.300
or L1.

01:10:54.300 --> 01:10:55.950
We could have
basically plugged in

01:10:55.950 --> 01:10:59.760
any differentiable distortion
metric, which even maybe maps

01:10:59.760 --> 01:11:02.730
human vision better or
human perception better.

01:11:02.730 --> 01:11:04.800
And actually, a lot of
work has gone into this.

01:11:04.800 --> 01:11:06.600
And we'll try to touch
a little bit on that

01:11:06.600 --> 01:11:08.430
in the next lecture, right?

01:11:08.430 --> 01:11:11.457
So you can basically
completely replace distortion.

01:11:11.457 --> 01:11:14.040
Remember, this is very hard to
do it in the traditional codecs

01:11:14.040 --> 01:11:16.470
because a lot of hand-designed
decisions were made,

01:11:16.470 --> 01:11:20.940
assuming you are optimizing
for MSE or PSNR, right?

01:11:20.940 --> 01:11:24.420
So these are like
really the main benefits

01:11:24.420 --> 01:11:27.920
of the learned image compressor.

01:11:27.920 --> 01:11:30.420
And let's just go back and see
whether this finished or not.

01:11:30.420 --> 01:11:31.920
Oh, it actually finished.

01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:34.170
And now you see reconstructions.

01:11:34.170 --> 01:11:36.780
If you look at number of
bits, you get much fewer bits.

01:11:36.780 --> 01:11:39.780
Like for each of them, you're
approximately getting 40 bits.

01:11:39.780 --> 01:11:41.820
So it indeed reduced the rate.

01:11:41.820 --> 01:11:45.580
But now you can see the quality
deteriorated at some places,

01:11:45.580 --> 01:11:46.080
right?

01:11:46.080 --> 01:11:50.340
So like, if I look at 8 and 5
here, and they were still bad,

01:11:50.340 --> 01:11:53.540
but it's better.

01:11:53.540 --> 01:11:55.370
Yeah, another thing
you see is that

01:11:55.370 --> 01:11:56.990
these ML-based
compressors-- they

01:11:56.990 --> 01:12:00.620
are doing a lot of low-pass
filtering like smoothening,

01:12:00.620 --> 01:12:02.330
which is somewhat,
again, we expect

01:12:02.330 --> 01:12:05.060
because it's hard to encode
the high-frequency components

01:12:05.060 --> 01:12:07.515
generally, right?

01:12:07.515 --> 01:12:08.015
OK.

01:12:11.810 --> 01:12:17.480
So last thing which I
just want to cover today

01:12:17.480 --> 01:12:19.115
is, so what are--

01:12:22.760 --> 01:12:24.530
what are some of the issues?

01:12:24.530 --> 01:12:26.195
And even actually before that--

01:12:29.780 --> 01:12:33.400
OK, let me--

01:12:33.400 --> 01:12:34.150
OK, sorry.

01:12:34.150 --> 01:12:35.800
Let's just look at this.

01:12:35.800 --> 01:12:38.260
Yeah, so this is
just a benchmark

01:12:38.260 --> 01:12:40.390
of how these compressors
perform in real life.

01:12:40.390 --> 01:12:43.480
So this is a paper from 2022.

01:12:43.480 --> 01:12:47.800
Here, different colors
and different compressors.

01:12:47.800 --> 01:12:51.460
The x-axis is bits per pixel,
so higher value of x-axis

01:12:51.460 --> 01:12:52.540
is higher rate.

01:12:52.540 --> 01:12:55.332
The y-axis is PSNR or MS-SSIM.

01:12:55.332 --> 01:12:56.290
Don't worry about them.

01:12:56.290 --> 01:12:57.520
These are just some metrics.

01:12:57.520 --> 01:13:00.610
Here, these are quality metrics
instead of loss metrics,

01:13:00.610 --> 01:13:02.560
so higher value is better.

01:13:02.560 --> 01:13:08.200
And what you see here is
like, this brown curve

01:13:08.200 --> 01:13:11.290
is JPEG, this guy here.

01:13:11.290 --> 01:13:16.510
OK, this pink one is
BPG, pink dashed one.

01:13:16.510 --> 01:13:17.680
So this is BPG.

01:13:17.680 --> 01:13:19.720
It's this guy, OK?

01:13:19.720 --> 01:13:21.975
And there is this
something called VVC/VTM,

01:13:21.975 --> 01:13:23.350
which is one of
the, I would say,

01:13:23.350 --> 01:13:25.090
state-of-the-art
traditional codec,

01:13:25.090 --> 01:13:27.820
which is actually
sitting somewhere here.

01:13:27.820 --> 01:13:29.590
I don't know if you
can see it clearly.

01:13:29.590 --> 01:13:30.730
Can you?

01:13:30.730 --> 01:13:34.790
Like let's see this
dash brown, OK?

01:13:34.790 --> 01:13:37.430
And this is like really
state of the art, even

01:13:37.430 --> 01:13:39.980
includes some ML-based
components in the middle

01:13:39.980 --> 01:13:41.840
for some small decision-making.

01:13:41.840 --> 01:13:46.142
But this is not an end-to-end
learned image compressor.

01:13:46.142 --> 01:13:47.600
It's still traditional
in the sense

01:13:47.600 --> 01:13:49.933
that it still have all the
traditional blocks, just uses

01:13:49.933 --> 01:13:52.940
something to learn a specific
block versus the blue one,

01:13:52.940 --> 01:13:57.020
which you see, is
what their work is,

01:13:57.020 --> 01:13:59.590
which is a learned
image compressor.

01:13:59.590 --> 01:14:01.870
And you can see,
in PSNR, basically,

01:14:01.870 --> 01:14:04.810
it's on the most on the top
left, which is what you want.

01:14:04.810 --> 01:14:07.180
You want higher quality
at lower bit rate.

01:14:07.180 --> 01:14:08.823
So it's performing best.

01:14:08.823 --> 01:14:10.240
So what I'm trying
to highlight is

01:14:10.240 --> 01:14:12.430
that these lower image
compresses are really

01:14:12.430 --> 01:14:13.390
state of the art.

01:14:13.390 --> 01:14:19.960
Like this VVC has almost done
5 to 10 years of research

01:14:19.960 --> 01:14:23.140
over various components,
and blocks, and everything,

01:14:23.140 --> 01:14:24.490
and, like, big teams.

01:14:24.490 --> 01:14:26.530
And then now, you have
learned compressor,

01:14:26.530 --> 01:14:27.970
which just comes and beats it.

01:14:27.970 --> 01:14:29.410
No more [INAUDIBLE]
optimization.

01:14:29.410 --> 01:14:31.270
So it's really cool
from that perspective,

01:14:31.270 --> 01:14:35.020
that given the amount of
effort, the output you can get

01:14:35.020 --> 01:14:36.460
is really crazy.

01:14:36.460 --> 01:14:37.840
And this is just PSNR.

01:14:37.840 --> 01:14:39.820
MS-SSIM is some
other metric, which

01:14:39.820 --> 01:14:43.030
is known to optimize, correlate
better with human vision.

01:14:43.030 --> 01:14:45.700
And there, the gap is
even higher, right?

01:14:45.700 --> 01:14:49.210
So here, you see BPG gives
you a lot of benefit.

01:14:49.210 --> 01:14:51.760
This guy gives you a little bit
more, and so on and so forth.

01:14:51.760 --> 01:14:53.230
A lot of these
traditional codecs

01:14:53.230 --> 01:15:00.170
are optimized for PSNR, which is
like MSC, just MSC in dB scale.

01:15:00.170 --> 01:15:03.770
But MS-SSIM, which is also by
the way, a lot of these codecs

01:15:03.770 --> 01:15:04.700
still look into.

01:15:04.700 --> 01:15:07.700
Even though it's a perceptual
metric, a lot of these codecs

01:15:07.700 --> 01:15:10.400
have still tried to
optimize for this, not JPEG.

01:15:10.400 --> 01:15:14.030
So you can see JPEG
is very far away.

01:15:14.030 --> 01:15:17.510
It's like this guy and all of
these guys versus this blue guy

01:15:17.510 --> 01:15:18.650
is like really beaten.

01:15:18.650 --> 01:15:21.370
So there's a lot of promise.

01:15:21.370 --> 01:15:23.680
So not only you can have
these domain-aware things.

01:15:23.680 --> 01:15:25.750
It's really at the forefront.

01:15:25.750 --> 01:15:26.830
It's state of the art.

01:15:34.970 --> 01:15:38.900
OK, so everything is green.

01:15:38.900 --> 01:15:43.460
Why is not-- why isn't there a
ML-based compressor everywhere?

01:15:43.460 --> 01:15:45.920
Why do we-- why do
we still studied

01:15:45.920 --> 01:15:49.190
like for 17 or [INAUDIBLE]
lectures of this class?

01:15:49.190 --> 01:15:51.680
Why didn't we start with this?

01:15:51.680 --> 01:15:55.370
One of the main issues with
ML-based encoder, decoder

01:15:55.370 --> 01:15:57.290
is speed [INAUDIBLE]

01:15:57.290 --> 01:15:59.580
Now, you have to train
this, for example,

01:15:59.580 --> 01:16:02.000
in a simple example,
million parameters,

01:16:02.000 --> 01:16:04.430
whereas in DCT or in
compression in general,

01:16:04.430 --> 01:16:07.520
I think really one point which
we hope we have driven home

01:16:07.520 --> 01:16:10.280
is that complexity is
a really big thing.

01:16:10.280 --> 01:16:12.950
Like it really makes or breaks
whether something is practical,

01:16:12.950 --> 01:16:14.330
whether something
will be used or not,

01:16:14.330 --> 01:16:16.400
whether something will
be deployed because these

01:16:16.400 --> 01:16:17.780
are like ubiquitous operations.

01:16:17.780 --> 01:16:19.238
They are just
running all the time.

01:16:19.238 --> 01:16:22.040
You need something efficient
which can run at speed, right?

01:16:22.040 --> 01:16:24.680
So this is just
like a screenshot

01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:28.070
from George Toderici's
talk at CVPR20.

01:16:28.070 --> 01:16:31.580
And what it shows here is
like H266 plus enhancement

01:16:31.580 --> 01:16:35.280
is, again, one of the more
advanced traditional codec,

01:16:35.280 --> 01:16:37.110
which is still more
computationally

01:16:37.110 --> 01:16:38.550
intensive than JPEG.

01:16:38.550 --> 01:16:41.790
But this is what ML-based
codecs were using back

01:16:41.790 --> 01:16:47.250
then, which was like almost 20x
more computational complexity.

01:16:47.250 --> 01:16:49.050
So now you can
imagine, right, like,

01:16:49.050 --> 01:16:51.240
what does 20x more
mean on the surface.

01:16:51.240 --> 01:16:53.400
It just feels like
it's 20, right?

01:16:53.400 --> 01:16:55.740
But no, it's like if
something was taking you

01:16:55.740 --> 01:16:59.550
one hour to encode, decode, now
it's going to take 20 hours.

01:16:59.550 --> 01:17:01.320
Something was taking
you one minute.

01:17:01.320 --> 01:17:02.820
It's going to take 20 minutes.

01:17:02.820 --> 01:17:04.710
Now, think you are
working on your computer,

01:17:04.710 --> 01:17:06.720
and you say, oh, this
thing-- one minute.

01:17:06.720 --> 01:17:07.230
I'll wait.

01:17:07.230 --> 01:17:08.610
This will decompress.

01:17:08.610 --> 01:17:11.280
Instead of that, now, if
you have to wait 20 minutes,

01:17:11.280 --> 01:17:13.500
will you use that technology?

01:17:13.500 --> 01:17:15.750
It's hard to justify that.

01:17:15.750 --> 01:17:21.360
So speed has really been one of
the areas where a lot of work

01:17:21.360 --> 01:17:22.760
goes into.

01:17:22.760 --> 01:17:25.050
You don't have to
worry about this.

01:17:25.050 --> 01:17:27.570
But even though
this is a blocker,

01:17:27.570 --> 01:17:30.930
I think one thing which we
should really take into account

01:17:30.930 --> 01:17:34.170
is that machine learning, deep
neural networks have really

01:17:34.170 --> 01:17:35.550
taken world by storm.

01:17:35.550 --> 01:17:38.418
And compression is not the
only application, right?

01:17:38.418 --> 01:17:39.960
Like there are tons
and tons and tons

01:17:39.960 --> 01:17:43.410
of application on your
phone or your devices

01:17:43.410 --> 01:17:45.300
to apply this, right?

01:17:45.300 --> 01:17:47.640
So a lot of work has
been really going

01:17:47.640 --> 01:17:50.040
on into hardware
support for neural net.

01:17:50.040 --> 01:17:51.570
And now you can
think if you have

01:17:51.570 --> 01:17:53.640
hardware support
for neural nets,

01:17:53.640 --> 01:17:55.710
then you can deploy
all of this much more

01:17:55.710 --> 01:17:58.720
efficiently and faster
on any devices you want.

01:17:58.720 --> 01:18:00.930
And this is happening
not only for compression,

01:18:00.930 --> 01:18:03.510
but to enable a
tons of applications

01:18:03.510 --> 01:18:05.990
which are out there.

01:18:05.990 --> 01:18:07.760
So this is happening anyways.

01:18:07.760 --> 01:18:10.220
And our compressors
can take advantage

01:18:10.220 --> 01:18:15.710
of this hardware lottery or
hardware boom in this area.

01:18:15.710 --> 01:18:17.930
By the way, we have
been talking about this.

01:18:17.930 --> 01:18:19.850
We are not going to
touch this in the class.

01:18:19.850 --> 01:18:23.120
I don't think any team
has picked up a project

01:18:23.120 --> 01:18:27.350
this time around this, but a
lot of these image and video

01:18:27.350 --> 01:18:29.450
compressors, which
we talked about,

01:18:29.450 --> 01:18:31.610
even traditional image
and video compressors

01:18:31.610 --> 01:18:34.340
actually have hardware
support in your computers.

01:18:34.340 --> 01:18:36.770
What that means is
Intel makes chip

01:18:36.770 --> 01:18:40.550
so that you can decode your
images or videos efficiently

01:18:40.550 --> 01:18:41.630
on the hardware.

01:18:41.630 --> 01:18:43.970
If you were just using
a commodity hardware,

01:18:43.970 --> 01:18:46.880
you wouldn't be able to actually
achieve the speeds which

01:18:46.880 --> 01:18:47.750
you would like.

01:18:47.750 --> 01:18:49.293
And again, what
this speech means

01:18:49.293 --> 01:18:50.960
is whether you're
going to watch YouTube

01:18:50.960 --> 01:18:54.260
or not, whether that circle of
death on YouTube, or Netflix,

01:18:54.260 --> 01:18:56.870
or whatever it is, whether
it's going to be one second,

01:18:56.870 --> 01:18:58.580
or whether it's going
to be one minute.

01:18:58.580 --> 01:19:00.860
So a lot of effort
goes into that.

01:19:00.860 --> 01:19:02.810
And so this is a problem
which is there even

01:19:02.810 --> 01:19:04.010
in traditional compressors.

01:19:04.010 --> 01:19:07.050
But already a lot of investment
and work has gone into it.

01:19:07.050 --> 01:19:08.490
And hopefully, in
neural nets also

01:19:08.490 --> 01:19:11.130
we are going to see the same.

01:19:11.130 --> 01:19:12.890
And yeah, so this is
like a huge AI chip

01:19:12.890 --> 01:19:15.050
landscape like thousands--

01:19:15.050 --> 01:19:17.210
not thousands, but
hundreds of companies

01:19:17.210 --> 01:19:19.040
working to make things better.

01:19:19.040 --> 01:19:21.740
Qualcomm-- OK, this is a
little bit older numbers,

01:19:21.740 --> 01:19:23.570
but basically Qualcomm
has a recent chip

01:19:23.570 --> 01:19:26.180
which does extremely
efficient ML inference.

01:19:26.180 --> 01:19:28.430
Today, we have M3, I
think, Apple chips,

01:19:28.430 --> 01:19:29.480
which are doing the same.

01:19:29.480 --> 01:19:31.730
Nvidia chips are coming
out for basically

01:19:31.730 --> 01:19:33.920
doing much more efficient
compression, decompression

01:19:33.920 --> 01:19:35.420
for learning stuff on GPUs.

01:19:35.420 --> 01:19:37.738
So a lot of stuff is
happening in this field.

01:19:37.738 --> 01:19:39.530
So even though this is
a problem right now,

01:19:39.530 --> 01:19:41.330
it's probably going
to be resolved.

01:19:41.330 --> 01:19:42.920
And the second
issue really is just

01:19:42.920 --> 01:19:47.000
determinism, which is
something to be careful about

01:19:47.000 --> 01:19:49.790
is like different
hardwares might have

01:19:49.790 --> 01:19:52.087
different floating point
operation implementations,

01:19:52.087 --> 01:19:54.170
and that could lead to
just catastrophic failures.

01:19:54.170 --> 01:19:56.180
Like CPU world has
really come together.

01:19:56.180 --> 01:19:58.340
I think GPU world is
still coming together.

01:19:58.340 --> 01:20:01.250
Every Nvidia update--
you have to be careful,

01:20:01.250 --> 01:20:03.110
and what devices you
are working with.

01:20:03.110 --> 01:20:04.742
So this is like really--

01:20:04.742 --> 01:20:06.200
even though this
doesn't sound big,

01:20:06.200 --> 01:20:08.360
this is really important
when you are working

01:20:08.360 --> 01:20:09.830
with this ML-based coders.

01:20:09.830 --> 01:20:11.670
Like whatever you
are doing should

01:20:11.670 --> 01:20:14.280
be reproducible across
machines and platforms.

01:20:14.280 --> 01:20:16.200
Otherwise, again,
you're not going

01:20:16.200 --> 01:20:20.010
to decode what you thought
you were going to decode.

01:20:20.010 --> 01:20:20.910
OK.

01:20:20.910 --> 01:20:25.610
And so this last slide from
today and then we are done.

01:20:25.610 --> 01:20:29.150
So these are basically, I guess,
the main takeaways from today.

01:20:29.150 --> 01:20:32.540
So ML-based codecs-- they allow
you for learned encoder-decoder

01:20:32.540 --> 01:20:35.820
transforms, better fidelity
to chosen probability models,

01:20:35.820 --> 01:20:36.320
right?

01:20:36.320 --> 01:20:39.170
So that basically gives you
the better rate-distortion.

01:20:39.170 --> 01:20:42.560
So this is a point which we
spent a significant time today.

01:20:42.560 --> 01:20:44.112
You can choose
whatever distortion

01:20:44.112 --> 01:20:46.070
you want as long as it's
differentiable, again,

01:20:46.070 --> 01:20:48.230
very powerful because it
allows you to just tomorrow

01:20:48.230 --> 01:20:49.647
if there is a
better vision model,

01:20:49.647 --> 01:20:51.560
you can make a better
compressor for that.

01:20:51.560 --> 01:20:55.280
Then you can have something
domain adaptable and flexible.

01:20:55.280 --> 01:20:59.570
The main idea to achieve the
ML-based things was like just

01:20:59.570 --> 01:21:01.820
be able to differentiate
across this quantization

01:21:01.820 --> 01:21:04.160
and discrete probability
models, and most likely

01:21:04.160 --> 01:21:05.840
are going to see more
and more ML-based

01:21:05.840 --> 01:21:07.280
codecs in your
future compressors.

01:21:07.280 --> 01:21:08.720
So very hot and active area.

01:21:08.720 --> 01:21:10.200
Participate in it.

01:21:10.200 --> 01:21:10.700
Cool.

01:21:10.700 --> 01:21:11.240
That's it.

01:21:11.240 --> 01:21:13.090
Thank you.

